#comment("\n\
The file is automatically generated by [atsdoc] from main.atxt.\n\
")
#comment("\n\
Time of Generation: #timestamp()\
")
<chapter
 id="lecture06-20">
#title("Through ATS to Javascript")

#para("\

Javascript (JS) is arguably the most popular programming language
on the plant Earth. In this chapter, I plan to demonstrate a style of
co-programming with ATS and JS.  In practice (of this style of
co-programming), ATS is mainly intended for high-level programming
(that, for instance, makes extensive use of combinators) and JS for
relatively low-level programming (that, for instance, handles direct
interactions with the browser running JS code).

")

#para("\

Let us take a look at a simple webpage for computing factorials: the file
#filename("Factorial.html") in the code of this chapter.
After inputing a natural number, one can click the
#emphasis('Evaluate') button to see some output mentioning the
factorial of the number.

Note that the following JS scripts (which are in the directory
#filename("contrib/libatscc2js") of the ATS2 repository) are needed for evaluating
JS code generated from ATS source:

<informalexample><programlisting>
&lt;script src=\"libatscc2js/CATS/basics_cats.js\"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;script src=\"libatscc2js/CATS/bool_cats.js\"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;script src=\"libatscc2js/CATS/integer_cats.js\"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;script src=\"libatscc2js/CATS/string_cats.js\"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;script src=\"libatscc2js/CATS/PRINT/print_store_cats.js\"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
</programlisting></informalexample>

The JS code in the file #filename("Factorial_dats.js") is generated
from compiling the ATS source stored in
#filename("Factorial.dats"). The command #command("patsopt") is called
on the ATS source to compile it onto C code, and the command
#command("atscc2js") (which is built by executing #command("make") in the
directory #filename("contrib/CATS-atscc2js") of the repository) is
subsequently called on the generated C code to
transpile it into JS code:

<informalexample><programlisting>
patsopt -o Factorial_dats.c -d Factorial.dats
atscc2js -o Factorial_dats.js -i Factorial_dats.c
</programlisting></informalexample>

")

#para("\

The JS backend has no garbage collector to rely on either, as the types of
ATS are the same: a linear list (or stream, or closure) in the ATS source has
to be consumed there, and the JS engine reclaims the memory of the JS values it
manages. In the example of this chapter nothing is linear.

")

#para("\

As a concrete example, please see the code in
#filename("Factorial.dats"). At the beginning of the file, the following code is
present:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
\#define ATS_MAINATSFLAG 1
\#define ATS_DYNLOADNAME "Factorial__dynload"
//
')

which indicates to #command("patsopt") that a dynload-function of the
name #dyncode("Factorial__dynload") is to be generated when the ATS
source contained in #filename("Factorial.dats") is compiled into C.
Then this dynload-function is transpiled into JS by
#command("atscc2js"), and it is supposed to be called first (to
perform initialization) before any function in the generated JS code
is put into use (the page calls it when it is loaded).

")

#para("\

The following lines in #filename("Factorial.dats")
are added for accessing the LIBATSCC2JS library, which is
needed for compiling ATS to JS:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
\#define
LIBATSCC2JS_targetloc
"$PATSHOME/contrib/libatscc2js"
//
\#include "{$LIBATSCC2JS}/mylibies.hats" // for prelude stuff
\#staload "{$LIBATSCC2JS}/SATS/print.sats" // for printing into a store
//
')

")

#para("\

In the following code,
a function of the name #dyncode("funarg1_get") is declared
in ATS and implemented in JS:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
funarg1_get(): int = "mac\#"
//
%{^
function funarg1_get()
{
  return parseInt(document.getElementById("funarg1").value);
}
%} (* end of external code *)
//
')

The function #dyncode("funarg1_get") essentially locates a
DOM-element identified by #dyncode("funarg1") and then converts
into an integer the string stored in the value-field of the
DOM-element.

Note that the use of the string #dyncode('"mac\#"') simply
indicates to #command("patsopt") that the function
#dyncode("funarg1_get") is to be given the same name when compiled
into C. In contrast, the name automatically chosen (by
#command("patsopt")) for #dyncode("funarg1_get") contains a long
prefix (for carrying some namespace information) if the string
#dyncode('"mac\#"') is not present. In case a different name is
needed for #dyncode("funarg1_get") in C, the name should be explicitly
mentioned after the symbol #dyncode('\#'). For instance, if the
string #dyncode('"mac\#JS_funarg1_get"') is used, then the name
#dyncode("JS_funarg1_get") is chosen (by #command("patsopt")) for
#dyncode("funarg1_get").  In ATS source, the code written between
#dyncode("%{^") and #dyncode("%}") is considered external and is
directly pasted by #command("patsopt") into the generated C code.  The
symbol #dyncode("^") means that the pasted code is to appear near the
beginning of the generated code. If the symbol #dyncode("^") is
omitted or replaced with #dyncode("$"), the code is to be pasted
near the bottom of the generated code.

")

#para("\

Another function implemented in JS puts a string into the DOM-element
identified by #dyncode("theOutput"):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
theOutput_set(text: string): void = "mac\#"
//
%{^
function theOutput_set(text)
{
  document.getElementById("theOutput").innerHTML = text;
}
%} (* end of external code *)
//
')

")

#para("\

The following function is called when the
#emphasis("Evaluate") button is clicked:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
Factorial__evaluate
  ((*void*)): void = "mac#"
//
implement
Factorial__evaluate
  ((*void*)) = let
  val () =
  the_print_store_clear()
  val arg = funarg1_get()
  val () =
  println!
  ("The factorial of ", arg, " is ", fact(arg))
  // end of [val]
in
  theOutput_set(the_print_store_join())
end // end of [Factorial__evaluate]
//
')

There is a global store, an array of strings, for receiving the
output generated from calling various print-functions. The function
#dyncode("the_print_store_clear") is for clearing this store and the
function #dyncode("the_print_store_join") for joining the strings
contained in the store into a single one.  The function
#dyncode("theOutput_set") updates the innerHTML-field of the
DOM-element identified by #dyncode("theOutput") with the string.

")

#para("\

The command #command("make") in the code of this chapter generates
#filename("Factorial_dats.js") from #filename("Factorial.dats"), and
#command("make regress") runs the generated code under Node.js with a tiny
stub of the DOM in place of a browser (the file #filename("test_Factorial.js")),
which checks that the output for 10 is the text
#dyncode("The factorial of 10 is 3628800"). The test is skipped
if #command("atscc2js") or #command("node") is not available. The page
itself needs a browser.

")

#para("\

For a more involved example, there was a webpage for animating the process that
searches (in the depth-first fashion) for solutions to the 8-queen puzzle,
and the code implementing depth-first search was simply adapted from the sample
ATS code presented in a previous chapter. That demo was written in the GC era
(it makes use of non-linear lists and streams) and it has not been ported: the
8-queen code of this book is linear and its stream-based version is presented in a
later chapter.

")

#para("\

Please find #mycodelink("LECTURE/06-20/CODE", "on-line") the entirety of
the code used in this chapter.

")#comment("para")

</chapter><!--id="lecture06-20"-->

